    "en":  {
               "headline":  "CRWD slides on AI selloff, then reported Zscaler talks; trend intact but tested",
               "technical":  "On 20 Nov 2026 CRWD closed at $229.44, down 5.76%, near the day\u0027s low of $227.30. The long-term trend is still up: the EMAs (exponential moving averages, which weight recent prices more) are stacked bullishly, with EMA50 $235.96 above EMA100 $221.09 above EMA200 $192.43. Short-term, though, price has slipped under the EMA50, so the next support is the EMA100 around $221. Momentum is mixed. MACD (12/26/9, a trend-momentum gauge) is negative at -4.31 but just above its signal line at -4.74, with a small positive histogram of +0.44. That is an early, still-fragile bullish crossover below zero. RSI 14 (a 0–100 momentum gauge; above 70 is overbought, below 30 is oversold) is 46.05, which is neutral. In the Bollinger Bands (20-day, ±2 standard deviations), price sits in the lower half, under the $238.11 midline. The bands run from $206.17 to $270.06. The stock is about 20% below its 52-week high of $286.99 and far above the $85.68 low. Volume of 12.8M was about 1.39× the 20-day average of 9.21M, so selling pressure was real. Overall posture: neutral, an uptrend under short-term pressure.",
               "fundamental":  "CrowdStrike\u0027s most recent quarter (Q2 FY27, reported 26 Aug 2026) was strong. Ending ARR (annual recurring revenue) grew 25% year over year to $5.84B, with a record $332.8M of net new ARR. ARR from Falcon Flex accounts more than doubled (+101%) to $2.29B, which suggests customers are adopting more modules on the single agent. Full-year FY27 ending-ARR guidance was raised to $6.603–6.612B. In the AI value chain, CRWD is positioning Falcon as the security layer for AI agents and AI factories. Partners in the last 30 days include NVIDIA, CoreWeave, Dell\u0027s AI Factory, AWS Bedrock AgentCore and Anthropic\u0027s Claude Cowork. Q3 FY27 results arrive on 1 Dec 2026 after the close, against guidance of $6.184–6.188B ending ARR.",
               "news":  [
                            "20 Nov 2026 (after the close): Reuters and Bloomberg reported that CrowdStrike is in talks to acquire Zscaler, which was valued at about $33.5B. ZS jumped about 17% after hours and CRWD fell about 8%. Neither company has confirmed the talks, and no deal is agreed.",
                            "20 Nov 2026 (regular session): CRWD fell 5.76% to $229.44 as an AI-related tech selloff deepened (Nasdaq -2.1% per Investing.com). The drop erased a two-day rally on 18–19 Nov.",
                            "18–19 Nov 2026: Cybersecurity stocks rallied after Palo Alto Networks reported record Q1 FY27 revenue of $3.44B, up 39% year over year. CRWD closed at $243.46 on 19 Nov.",
                            "17–18 Nov 2026: CEO George Kurtz sold 20,000 shares (about $4.5M) under a pre-arranged 10b5-1 trading plan. Routine, but it added to cautious sentiment.",
                            "3 Nov 2026 (Fal.Con Europe): CrowdStrike launched an AI Kill Switch in Falcon Guardian to stop rogue AI agents. It also announced plans to acquire Fiddler (AI agent observability) and Dreadnode (automated AI security testing), and said EY committed to Falcon.",
                            "21 Oct – 2 Nov 2026: CrowdStrike announced a Falcon Guardian integration with NVIDIA, a partnership to secure CoreWeave\u0027s AI cloud, and integrated security for the Dell AI Factory with NVIDIA."
                        ],
               "caution":  "The $229.44 close does not include the roughly 8% after-hours drop on 20 Nov, so the stock could open near the low $210s. That would be below the EMA100 ($221.09) and close to the lower Bollinger Band ($206.17). A Zscaler deal would likely be the largest in CRWD\u0027s history. It could mean share dilution or debt, a long integration of a very different network-proxy architecture, and less management focus right before Q3 results on 1 Dec. AI-sector valuations are also under pressure across the market.",
               "opportunity":  "If the deal happens, Zscaler would fill CrowdStrike\u0027s biggest gap, network and zero-trust access. That would let CRWD offer endpoint, cloud, identity and network security from one vendor and compete head-on with Palo Alto Networks, while customers are actively cutting the number of security vendors they use. The underlying business is strong: ARR is growing 25% and Falcon Flex ARR is doubling. The bullish EMA stack and the fresh MACD crossover show the longer uptrend has not broken yet.",
               "risks":  [
